[Applause]  [Music]  for once the predictions became true  french president emmanuel macron not  only won but crushed his rival marianne  le pen in the second round of the french  presidential election we are talking  about no less than 16 points of  difference approximately 58 percent from  macron and 42 for le pen yes the margin  was smaller than the previous  presidential elections in which these  two politicians also faced each other  and in which the difference was 32  points but we are still talking about a  huge difference in a country where  re-election is not exactly common  macron's victory marks the first time in  20 years that a french president has won  a second term and the first in more than  50 years that he has done so by  completely dominating political life  without having to work with a government  and a prime minister of a different  party elected by the national assembly  the first time in more than 50 years  you can't deny that it's all a bit  political even more so if we take into  account that the re-election has taken  place after a pandemic a crisis and in  the midst of a huge inflationary spiral  and on top of that the absence rate was  enormous and what can i say when you  want to change or prefer the alternative  you do not stay at home in other words  we can almost speak of a new sun king  for french politics he is not the most  beloved leader and has a reputation for  authoritarianism but he has command in  term and yes the most shocking thing of  all is that the french are not  particularly fond of him in opinion  polls he is regularly accused of being  arrogant authoritarian and snobbish i'm  sure photos like this one probably don't  help  and not only that we can't even say that  his political proposals seem to dazzle  the masses  in the first round almost 60 percent of  french voters supported the extreme  right or extreme left candidates in fact  his victory was accompanied by an  abstention rate of 28 points also the  highest and half a century in the second  round it is clear that macron does not  arouse passions but then why has macron  made a clean sweep where is french  politics headed and what can we expect  in his new term at the helm of france  well let's take a look  [Music]  why macron  [Music]  the french leader has managed france  with a firm hand in 2017 he came to  power under a banner of neither right  nor left just himself trying to shape a  new france throughout his first term  there were times when his presidency  seemed closed to the end yet he always  bounced back again and again take for  example the case of the yellow vests the  largest protests in decades or even the  covet 19 crisis where at the beginning  the friends were among the most opposed  to vaccines today 78 of the population  is fully vaccinated a higher percentage  than in germany the united kingdom or  the united states but above all he has  tried to modernize france and when we  talk about modernizing the gaelic  country we are talking about its economy  to give you an idea france's economy has  grown an average of one percent over the  past 10 years and if instead of looking  at the economy as a whole we look at gdp  per capita well i think a picture here  is worth a thousand words pay attention  to this chart  in international dollars the french have  been losing wealth for more than 14  years and even if we eliminate the  effect of exchange rates and price  variations the result is not much better  the output of the french economy barely  increased by six percent in the entire  decade prior to the pandemic this is a  much worse record than that of most  developed countries  in other words as you can see the french  economy has been practically in a coma  if we were to describe its figures over  the last decade even being generous we  would say that they are disappointing we  are talking about a deficit of more than  six percent of gdp a public debt of more  than 112 an exorbitant public  expenditure of no less than 55 of gdp  and practically no growth in recent  years it is difficult to find any  statistic where things are going really  well in fact it occurs to me that a good  indicator might be industrial production  which in developed countries is an  all-time high and france has  traditionally been a very industrial  country so the question is how has  france's industrial production evolved  well once again i think a picture is  worth a thousand words  in order to address this macron has made  efforts to deregulate the economy and  reduce taxes efforts that have been very  well received by the business community  and have for example made it possible  for unemployment to fall to seven  percent the lowest level since 2008. his  achievements also include the fact that  france is having the fastest post  covered 19 recovery of the major  european economies in fact as you can  see in this chart it was the only major  economy to have recovered to its  pre-pandemic level by the end of 2021  among his first measures after being  sworn in as president in 2017 was to  abolish the wealth tax and among his  greatest achievements the reduction of  eight points in the corporate tax rate  along with the labour reform facility  hiring and firing are unusually  highlighted perhaps this is why it is  not surprising that according to polls  almost 8 out of 10 business executives  voted for the french president but in  any case we're talking about small  changes at least much smaller than what  the french economy really needs but they  have worked for the time being and that  is the line of work he has now pledged  to follow among his promises are to  reduce production taxes for companies  and the self-employed by seven billion  euros cut inheritance tax and achieve a  deficit of three percent of gdp by 2027.  in other words discrete adjustments and  five more years of wild spending  [Music]  since the start of the war in ukraine  alone the french government has spent 22  billion euros that's 23.5 billion us  dollars to compensate consumers and  businesses for gas electricity and  gasoline increases all coming out of the  public coffers and indeed estimates  suggest that mccron's promises far from  stemming the bleeding will add 44  billion euros annually to the french  government deficits if he keeps his  promises that is whatever the case what  is expected is a much more aggressive  international role macron wants to put  paris in the driver's seat in brussels  for instance in recent months we have  seen how he has wanted to play a very  very key role in the ukrainian crisis  even if it has not worked out  particularly well how could good old  emmanuel think that he would not be able  to make vladimir putin see reason as it  stands it's now taken for granted that  the french government will support  europe establishing an oil embargo on  russia but do you know what the ukraine  crisis has shown as one of the most  volatile and fickle parts of his  character this is perhaps the worst side  of the french president the eternal  opportunism of paris check this out  [Music]  the most opportunistic aspect of the  french president  [Music]  over and above everything else  visualpolitik fans macron has been a  lucky guy with enough agility to move  his ships off course when the wind was  blowing in a different direction than  the one he wanted to travel in the last  example was the russian invasion of  ukraine or rather during the run-up to  the invasion as we told you in our  weekly patreon bulletin and also in a  past video here on the channel macron  wanted to take advantage of the tension  on the ukrainian border to gain some  distance from washington claim a  european position of his own and  negotiate with putin whilst also taking  revenge for the affront suffered with  occus when the australian government  cancelled a multi-million dollar  submarine contract with the french  military industry in order to make a  deal with the united states and the  united kingdom and that was not the  worst thing the worst thing is that  paris basically found out about it from  the press there see how little is left  of france's influence  [Music]  at the time the french president said  things like this  europeans must stop being naive we have  to respond and show that we have the  strength and the capacity to defend  ourselves emmanuel macron so when things  in ukraine got red hot macron saw the  perfect opportunity to kick the us  government in the teeth that's why in  the european parliament itself he came  out with this  it is good for the europeans and the  united states to coordinate but it is  necessary for the europeans to conduct  their own dialogue emmanuel macron  presidents of france to the european  parliament no common position no  reinforcement of nato or anything like  that the european union had to go it  alone to negotiate with putin of course  it convinced no one while paris called  for european unity warsaw tallinn riga  vilnius prague stockholm helsinki  copenhagen bratislava virtually all  europe's capitals began to look to  washington and nato it was the best  possible protection the only possible  protection and to top it all off then  came the invasion itself it was clear  that a military response was the only  viable one and there nato wins by a  landslide macron's plan went out the  window  [Music]  and you know what he did next he quickly  did a 180 degree turn a turn that has in  a certain way also played a leading role  during his campaign of the second round  when he adopted some positions of the  left such as the slowing down the  increase of the retirement age or  turning to the greens exactly the  opposite of what his own economic  diagnosis indicates  macron waters down key pension reform in  a bid to woo the left evidently power is  power and france is france we are not  going to blame it for its own nature nor  expect pairs from an oak tree the  problem is that these questions reflect  the major shortcomings of the french  president macron has contributed to  improving france but he's not brought  about the radical change he promised nor  does he seem ready to do so now macron's  victory means continuity in economic  policy and also in foreign policy and  yes at this point i'm going to make a  personal statement although the results  so far have been rather poor it seems  clear that he was by far the best option  and i'm not only talking about marianne  le pen though that is a lot of it the  truth is that we could almost say that  the french politics as a whole is  [Music]  adrift politics of extremes  [Music]  pay attention to these statements  as soon as the russian ukrainian war is  over and settled by a peace treaty i  will call for the implementation of a  strategic reproachment between nato and  russia it is in the interests of france  and europe as well as of the united  states marine le pen  that's a statement that is better  understood when we take into account  this other promise  le pen promises to pull france out of  nato allied command if she wins  presidential election huh how would that  work we wonder le pen continues to see  france as a world superpower a  superpower that has to reclaim its place  in the world and to do so she sees  nothing better than to rebel against  nato and the european union in favor of  pushing for protectionist policies to  get closer to moscow and to juggle  economically because le pen promised big  tax cuts but also big spending increases  and what can i say that is not usually a  good combination it smells more like  populism than anything else like when  she says she wants to fight inflation by  punishing international trade and  lowering vat which would boost household  demand even more and with it prices  well you know how everything looks good  on paper to give you an idea it has been  quantified that le pen's promises would  mean an additional deficit of 105  billion dollars to reach a total hole of  226 billion dollars per year now you  know how to make france great france's  debt that is  [Music]  among her proposals we also found things  like re-establishing the wealth tax  nationalizing the freeway network  lowering the retirement age to 60 and  paying families up to 100 000 euros in  exchange for having at least three  children visualpolitik fans during the  18th century this country gave the world  concepts of left and right now it is  also leading a new transition of  political camps that the analyst gideon  rackman describes as nationalists versus  internationalists between those who fear  globalization and those who embrace it  as an opportunity  there is no more left or right the real  division is between the patriots and the  globalists marine le pen of course that  is limiting and oversimplifying the  political spectrum be that as it may  despite the result the extreme right of  le pen and company is stronger than ever  and indeed it was ahead amongst the  young and the working class nationalism  euroscepticism and rejection of  immigration are gaining more and more  followers  [Music]  and on the left pretty much the same  story the fact that the leader of the  left block is a candidate as radical as  melancholy does not inspire much  confidence either french society seems  increasingly divided and polarized and  if things continue to go from bad to  worse in france the outcome may soon be  very different in the first round of  these elections the historic centre-left  was left with two percent of the votes  and the centre-right did not reach a  meager five percent the political  spectrum was divided between macron's  center and the extremes  macron's biggest challenge will be to  create a sense of cohesion in an  extremely fragmented country tara varma  european council on foreign relations  but at least for now there are five  years of continuity  [Music]  this is precisely why the re-election of  the french president has been greeted  with loud cheers by virtually all  international leaders from biden to  zelinski himself to the president of the  european commission ursula van der  leyden congratulations to emmanuel  macron on his re-election france is our  oldest ally and a key partner in  addressing global challenges i look  forward to our continued close club  operation including on supporting  ukraine defending democracy and  countering climate change be that as it  may a new battle is now brewing what is  known as the third round macron needs to  win the june national assembly elections  to be able to rule as he pleases and  what can i say given what we have seen  let's hope he succeeds macron is not  proving to be a revolutionary president  but at least he has managed to turn the  rusty rudder of french politics yes it  may not be enough but something is  something and amongst the alternatives  there seems to be nothing but old  formulas that are more reminiscent of  the old populist messages of latin  america than the more prosperous  countries it's clear that france needs  many changes economy pensions security  in any case what was surprising were the  results of the elections in the french  overseas territories they were  completely different from those of the  metropolis in the parts of france that  are scattered far away from paris marion  le pen has performed particularly well  winning in places such as reunion  guadalupe french goanna and martinique  among many others and some of them  moreover by a wide margin what is  happening in overseas france and why  does it seem so different from the  european continent well visualpolitik  fans this is something we will talk  about in an upcoming video here on  visualpolitik so don't forget to  subscribe to the channel if you haven't  already done so also tell us what do you  think about the results of the french  elections what is your opinion of  emmanuel macron do you think france will  manage to get back on track leave us  your answer i